  abstract = {The dc and ac conductivities of carbon nanotubespolyepoxy composites have been investigated from 20 to 110 oC in the frequency range 10-2106 Hz as a function of the conductive weight fraction p ranging from 0.04 to 2.5 wt \%. The frequency dependence of the measured conductivity obeys the universal dynamic response (UDR): a dc plateau followed, above a critical frequency omegac, by the omegas power law with exponent s 0.61. The dc conductivity follows a percolation scaling law: sigmadc (p pc)t with pc = 0.3 wt \% and t = 1.41.8, according to the temperature. sigmadc reached 10-4 S/cm for 2.5 wt \% CNTs content and increases with increasing temperature. Considering a biased random walk in three dimensions approach, we may explain the scaling law of omegac with p and its proportionality to sigmadc. The universality of ac conduction in carbon nanotubespolymer composites is examined by the construction of master curves.},
