  url = {http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.4331},
  urldate = {2010-12-15},
  abstract = {We prove that for each \$k\textbackslash ge0\$, the probability that a root vertex in a random planar graph has degree \$k\$ tends to a computable constant \$d\_k\$, so that the expected number of vertices of degree \$k\$ is asymptotically \$d\_k n\$, and moreover that \$\textbackslash sum\_k d\_k =1\$. The proof uses the tools developed by Gimenez and Noy in their solution to the problem of the asymptotic enumeration of planar graphs, and is based on a detailed analysis of the generating functions involved in counting planar graphs. However, in order to keep track of the degree of the root, new technical difficulties arise. We obtain explicit, although quite involved expressions, for the coefficients in the singular expansions of the generating functions of interest, which allow us to use transfer theorems in order to get an explicit expression for the probability generating function \$p(w)=\textbackslash sum\_k d\_k w\textasciicircum k\$. From this we can compute the \$d\_k\$ to any degree of accuracy, and derive the asymptotic estimate \$d\_k \textbackslash sim c\textbackslash cdot k\textasciicircum\textbraceleft -1/2\textbraceright{} q\textasciicircum k\$ for large values of \$k\$, where \$q \textbackslash approx 0.67\$ is a constant defined analytically.},
  archiveprefix = {arXiv},
  keywords = {05A16 05C30,Mathematics - Combinatorics},
  file = {/home/count0/stuff/workspace/pesquisa/zotero/storage/Z9EWUKCA/Drmota et al. - 2009 - Degree distribution in random planar graphs.pdf;/home/count0/stuff/workspace/pesquisa/zotero/storage/7D9FXW9M/0911.html}
