  urldate = {2012-12-18},
  abstract = {In the modeling, monitoring, and control of complex networks, a fundamental problem concerns the comprehensive determination of the state of the system from limited measurements. Using power grids as example networks, we show that this problem leads to a new type of percolation transition, here termed a network observability transition, which we solve analytically for the configuration model. We also demonstrate a dual role of the network's community structure, which both facilitates optimal measurement placement and renders the networks substantially more sensitive to ``observability attacks.'' Aside from their immediate implications for the development of smart grids, these results provide insights into decentralized biological, social, and technological networks.},
